    title_en: "Japanese Entertainment System Manufacturer Nippon Columbia Infected with Malware, Exposing Data of Over 8.6 Million Customers and Employees",
    summary_en: "Major Japanese entertainment system manufacturer Daiichi Kosho announced that the systems of its outsourcing contractor, Nippon Columbia Group (NCG), were infected with malware. The company is Japan's largest karaoke manufacturer, serving well-known chains such as Big Echo. NCG is responsible for handling personal information for 93,000 employees and 8,631,000 customers. The exposed data includes sensitive personal information such as full names, gender, dates of birth, email addresses, and phone numbers. Although Daiichi Kosho has not confirmed any data leakage or misuse, it has advised customers to remain highly vigilant, especially regarding emails, text messages, or phone calls requesting money or sensitive information. The company emphasized that the exposed data does not include passwords, and there is currently no evidence that membership points have been used without authorization. NCG has reset passwords and various authentication credentials, and is investigating the root cause, scope of the incident, and whether the data has been leaked online. Affected brands include BIG ECHO and MEGA BIG. Practical advice suggests users increase their vigilance and be wary of any suspicious requests for personal information.",
