    title_en: "PC Shipments Plunge 20% Amid High Memory and Storage Costs, Windows 11 Updates Focus on Optimizing Low-Spec Devices",
    summary_en: "According to data from Omdia and IDC, global PC shipments are projected to decline by approximately 20% in Q3 2026, marking the steepest annual drop since Q1 2023. The primary cause of the market slump is the significant price increase of core components such as DRAM (memory) and SSDs (solid-state drives), causing their cost share to jump from about 15% to nearly 40%, severely impacting the overall pricing structure of PCs. Furthermore, early inventory hoarding by OEM manufacturers has disrupted traditional quarterly seasonal demand. Despite facing weak demand and high cost pressures, Windows 11 continues to undergo optimization, particularly for low-spec (e.g., 8GB RAM) PCs, with versions like 26H2 improving startup and File Explorer speeds. However, the market predicts that PC prices will remain high, and the downward demand cycle may persist.",
